/* book exam traps: Dose is per area (cm⁻²); concentration is per volume (cm⁻³). A 10¹⁵ cm⁻² implant spread over 0.1 μm gives about 10²⁰ cm⁻³, not 10¹⁵ cm⁻³. | L = 2√(Dt) is not the junction depth. The junction is where the dopant falls to the background doping, xj = L·√ln(Cs/NB), usually two to three times L. | Wet oxide is fast and thick, dry oxide is slow and dense. Gate oxide is dry, field oxide is wet — not the other way round. | Deal-Grove: linear for thin oxide (reaction-limited), parabolic for thick (diffusion-limited). Examiners deliberately swap the two labels. */
